# Angular coding style

Narrows the stack-agnostic style rules to current Angular idiom (standalone
components, signals, `inject()`). Everything not Angular-specific comes from
the common rules this file `extends`.

## Components and files

- Components, directives, and pipes are standalone: do not set `standalone: false` or write a new `NgModule` unless the project is NgModule-based end-to-end.
- One component per file, named after the class (`OrderSummary` in `order-summary.ts`, not `component.ts`). Match the project's file-suffix convention (`.component.ts` vs plain `.ts`); do not introduce a second one.
- Extract a child component when a template passes roughly 60-80 lines or branches (`@if`/`@for`) for more than one concern.

## Dependency injection

- Prefer `inject()` over constructor-parameter injection for new code (`private readonly router = inject(Router);`). Pick one style per class; do not mix.
- Call `inject()` only in an injection context (constructor, field initializer, `runInInjectionContext`), not in `setTimeout` or a plain async callback.

## Signals and state

- Use `signal()` for local state, `computed()` for derived values, `input()`/`model()` for inputs; change a signal only through `.set()`/`.update()`.
- Keep `computed()` pure (no side effects, no writes to other signals); use `effect()` for side effects.
- Do not mirror a signal into a plain class field for the template; read `value()` directly.

## Templates and control flow

- Use `@if`, `@for` (with `track`), `@switch` in new templates, not `*ngIf`/`*ngFor`/`*ngSwitch`; in a template that still uses the structural-directive form throughout, match the file.
- Always give `@for` an explicit `track` on the item's stable id, not `$index`, unless the list has no natural identity.

## Naming and typing

- Name a signal for the value it holds (`user`, not `userSignal`).
- Type every `input()`/`output()`/`model()` explicitly (`input<Order | null>(null)`).
- Avoid `any` in component/service code; narrow loosely-typed CLI-generated values (a form's raw value) at the boundary.
